Glyn Kennington wrote:
> So if a player dies when polyed into a troll and wearing a
> "oUnchanging, the corpse should presumably have a chance of resurrecting
> (as "the ice troll called GetSome") if loaded as a bones file?

Unlike flesh-ified statues, player corpses in bones filed aren't named.
Therefore, they aren't "called" after the deceased when revived unless
you name the corpse yourself before reviving it. This holds true even
if they died in monster form.

Regardless, I tried this just to see what it would do. The results
were...amusing. Player troll corpses *do* revive. But there's an odd
twist if the deceased player's ghost is still present.

If you can see the troll corpse but not the ghost, then you simply get
the usual:
   "The troll rises from the dead!"

If you can see the ghost, however, you receive this message:
   "<So-and-so's> ghost is suddenly drawn into its former body!"

If you can also see the troll, the usual troll regeneration message is
suppressed. The ghost message is the same one you get if you revive a
player corpse with a wand of undead turning while in view of their
un-vanquished ghost.

It's surprisingly consistant behavior. Either TDT really does TOE, or
else they just recycled the undead turning code for troll auto-revival
(or maybe vice versa). Either way, it's amusing.



